    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Local Artist.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Assist in explaining the rules of a classroom game. Concept: Guide peers through setting up a classroom activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist in explaining the rules of an art-related game with basic actions (e.g., gesturing "Draw something blue").
            Can guide peers through setting up a classroom activity by demonstrating steps (e.g., showing how to fold paper for "First, fold the paper").
            Can provide support by explaining each part of a craft activity (e.g., points to the markers and says, "Now add colors here").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing pictures of different art materials (e.g., a canvas, paintbrushes, clay, paper, fabric for molas). Students quickly identify them by saying the name (e.g., "This is clay!"). Teacher then asks, "What can you **create** with these materials?" to elicit prior vocabulary related to "Arts and Crafts".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a simple craft step using imperatives. For example, holding up a piece of paper: "First, **fold** the paper in half. Then, **cut** a small shape. See? Now you have a **unique** design." The teacher uses gestures and clear **pronunciation** of words like 'design' (/dɪ'zaɪn/, stress on second syllable) and demonstrates the action. This models how to guide peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you just listen, or do you also watch my hands when I explain steps?" (Expected answer: "Watch and listen!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it important to speak clearly when explaining something to a friend?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated set of instructions for a basic Panamanian craft, like making a mini **mola** or a tagua carving illustration (simplified). The text will describe the steps using **imperatives** and introduce target vocabulary. Example text: "How to Make a Paper Mola. First, **prepare** three different **colorful** pieces of paper. Next, **cut** out a geometric shape from the top paper. Then, **glue** it onto the second paper. Finally, **add** more details."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Propose and develop steps for art projects in class while engaging in feedback exchanges.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the instructions silently.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main purpose of these instructions?" (Expected answer: "To show us how to make a craft.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sequencing Activity:</b> Students receive mixed-up steps from the instructions on cards. In pairs, they order the steps correctly, then **mediate** by verbally explaining their chosen order to their partner using phrases like "**First**, you do this. **Next**, you do that."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on using **imperatives** to explain steps, **present perfect** to describe achievements, **adjectives** for describing art, and art-related **vocabulary** (artist, craft, mola, sculpture, create, design, colorful, unique). Emphasis on **pronunciation** of syllable stress (e.g., sculpture, design) and **inflectional endings** (-ed, -ing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Explanation):</b> Teacher provides a handout with sentences describing a simple craft, but with missing **imperative verbs** (e.g., "**____** the paper in half. **____** a circle."). Students fill in the blanks, then **mediate** by reading their completed sentences to a partner, ensuring clarity and correct verb usage. Teacher monitors for accurate **inflectional endings** (e.g., 'folding' vs 'folded').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled (Guided Explanation):</b> In pairs, students receive pictures depicting sequential steps of a different art activity (e.g., planting a small seed, decorating a simple picture frame). One student describes a step using an **imperative** (e.g., "**First, paint** the frame blue."), and the other student asks a clarifying question or repeats for confirmation (e.g., "So, **paint** the frame?"). This encourages **sociolinguistic** feedback exchange. Teacher prompts students to use relevant **adjectives** (e.g., "**bold**, **vibrant** colors").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Mediation of Artist Info):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a short profile of a Panamanian **artist** (e.g., "Olga Sinclair is an artist. She **has created** many **abstract** paintings."). One student reads a sentence about the artist's life or achievements (**present perfect** or **past simple**), and the other student must rephrase it or summarize it simply for a "younger sibling" (a mediation task). Example: "She **has designed** a beautiful **mural** in the city." Partner: "So, she made a big, pretty painting on a wall." Teacher reinforces correct **syllable stress** in words like 'sculpture' and 'design'.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Art History Project Explanation. Students, in groups of three, take turns explaining a specific aspect of their "Art History Presentation" project to their group members. Since this is Lesson 5, the project should be near completion or planned out.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connect to Project:</b> Each student will focus on mediating one of the following to their peers:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">a) Explaining the main **craft** or art form their researched **artist** makes (e.g., "My artist, artisans from Emberá, **create** **baskets** from natural fibers. First, you **prepare** the materials and **soak** the fibers."). Using **imperatives** and **describing creations**.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">b) Summarizing 2-3 key achievements of their artist using the **present perfect** (e.g., "This artist **has created** many **unique sculptures**. He **has also displayed** his work in a **gallery**."). Using **describing achievements of artists**.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">c) Guiding peers through a specific step they plan to use in *their own* presentation creation (e.g., "First, **draw** the main picture on your **canvas**. Then, **add** **colorful** details with markers."). This involves **guiding peers through setting up a classroom activity** (the presentation itself).</li>
                </ul>
                Students must use gestures and clear language to ensure their peers understand. Teacher reminds them to use **adjectives** like 'vibrant', 'bold', 'detailed' to describe the art.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on effective mediation skills and use of target language. Peers also complete a simple "Peer Feedback Card" for the student who explained.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student selects one key idea or step from their "Art History Presentation" (e.g., "How an artist makes a **mola**" or "What their chosen artist **has achieved**"). They stand up and briefly explain this idea/step to the entire class (or a small group if time is limited), ensuring clarity and using appropriate gestures. They must use at least one **imperative** and one sentence with **present perfect** or **past simple**. They might point to a picture or prop if available (e.g., "Now **add** colors here" while pointing to a drawing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible **pronunciation** and appropriate **syllable stress** (e.g., sculpture, design).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear use of **target vocabulary** (e.g., artist, craft, sculpture, create, design, colorful, unique).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of **imperatives** and **present perfect** / **past simple** to explain or describe.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation and ability to guide/assist peers (effective mediation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in feedback exchanges (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small card:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you explained well to your classmates today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new **artistic** word you used today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did explaining your art project to others help you understand it better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned from a classmate's explanation today?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of mediating instructions or project details worked well? Did students use the **present perfect** and **imperatives** accurately? Were the chosen art-related **vocabulary** words used naturally? What adjustments are needed for the next time students need to explain or guide their peers, perhaps by providing more sentence starters or visual aids? Were students comfortable giving and receiving **sociolinguistic** feedback? I noted strong usage of **adjectives** to describe art.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "This Is a Local Artist" theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, but the skills of explaining, guiding, and presenting will be transferable to new contexts and projects.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>